    {
      "id": "eu-design-md",
      "x": 100,
      "y": 780,
      "width": 420,
      "height": 480,
      "type": "markdown",
      "file": "/content/boards/projects/eurocrate-storage/design-notes.md",
      "href": "/content/boards/projects/eurocrate-storage/design-notes.md",
      "title": "Design notes",
      "_rawMarkdown": "# Design notes\n\nA steel welded frame carrying eurocrates as drawers. The crates stay standard\n600x400, so they remain cheap to source second hand and easy to replace.\n\n## Sliding\n\n- HDPE strips on the sliding surfaces\n- Teflon on the friction points if HDPE alone is not slippery enough\n- A thin sheet over the top surface so dust does not settle into an open crate\n\n## Stability\n\n- Ballast in the base, sand or klinker blocks\n- The base has to outweigh the loaded drawers: 6 crates, 20kg maximum each\n- Heavy duty castors, locking\n\n## Small parts\n\nEuroPlus flex organizers inside a crate for anything too small to sit loose.\n\n## Sizes in use\n\nThe 600x400 footprint comes in three heights, 75mm, 170mm and 270mm. The\n400x300x120 is the half-footprint for lighter things. Supplier links are on the\nboard next to this note.\n",
      "markdownId": "cosmo-note-eu-design-notes",
      "markdownUpdatedAt": "2026-07-29T11:00:00.000Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "eu-open-md",
      "x": 540,
      "y": 780,
      "width": 420,
      "height": 480,
      "type": "markdown",
      "file": "/content/boards/projects/eurocrate-storage/open-questions.md",
      "href": "/content/boards/projects/eurocrate-storage/open-questions.md",
      "title": "Open questions",
      "_rawMarkdown": "# Open questions\n\n## Anti-tip interlock\n\nWorth building at all? The failure case is several loaded drawers open at once\npulling the whole frame over.\n\n- Engages once a crate moves even slightly out\n- Releases when the crate is removed completely, since a crate that is out of\n  the frame cannot tip it\n- Is \"only one open at a time\" the right rule, or too restrictive to live with?\n\n## Sourcing\n\nSecond hand supply is the whole reason for standardising on 600x400. Are\nMarktplaats and liquidation sales reliable enough to count on, or does emtrade\nend up being the default anyway?\n\n## Weight budget\n\nConfirm the base ballast beats a fully loaded, fully extended set of drawers\nbefore committing to a frame design. That number decides how much mass goes in\nthe base, which decides whether it can be moved at all.\n\n## Why not just boxes\n\nWorth answering honestly before building anything. Stacked boxes are free. The\nframe only earns its place if getting to the bottom crate stops being a chore.\n",
      "markdownId": "cosmo-note-eu-open-questions",
      "markdownUpdatedAt": "2026-07-29T11:00:00.000Z"
    },
